=====================================================================
PART F — REMAINING GENUINELY UNOBSERVABLE POINTS (do not over-specify)
=====================================================================
1. `port >= 0` vs `port != -1` vs `0 <= port <= 65535`: provably indistinguishable,
   since normalization makes -1 the only value outside 0..65535 that port() can report
   (confirmed over 156 port values incl. every value in -5..5 and 65525..65545).
2. Whether the clamp-to--1 lives in the constructor or in port()/to_string(): the two
   accessors agreed in 100% of 156 port cases; only the combined effect is observable.
3. Whether the leading-'/' fix-up lives in construction or is re-applied inside
   to_string(): idempotent, hence unobservable. Implement it once at construction.
4. Whether an unreachable userinfo branch exists in to_string(): no input can populate
   userinfo (no arg slot; extra argv ignored), and '@' appears in output only when host
   contains it. Implement no userinfo at all.
5. Behavior for a port string that makes stoi throw: the process dies before printing,
   so to_string() is undefined/unreachable for those inputs.
6. NUL bytes cannot be delivered through argv, so byte 0x00 in any field is untestable.
